        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Constitutional monarchy</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1689 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Shared under a constitution: in most, an elected parliament and a cabinet answerable to it govern while the monarch acts on ministers’ advice; in some the monarch keeps real executive powers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hereditary right bound by law: the crown’s authority rests on a constitution, statutes and conventions, the government’s on the consent of voters expressed through parliament</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">The monarch by hereditary succession regulated by law, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a>; ministers through parliamentary elections and the confidence of the legislature</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Famous examples</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> (1707–1800), Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> under the Constitution of 1814, Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> under the Constitution of 1831, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> (1871–1918), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a>, Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> since 1978</span></div>
