    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Constitutional monarchy keeps a hereditary head of state but binds the crown by law; in its parliamentary form the monarch reigns while elected ministers govern.</li><li>It took shape in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a> and spread through 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, Siam and the self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</li><li>After two world wars swept away many thrones, the surviving monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> almost all adopted the British model of a ceremonial crown and parliamentary government.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>No British monarch has refused royal assent to a bill since 11 March 1708, when Queen Anne withheld it from the Scottish Militia Bill; the power has never been abolished but is widely thought to have lapsed.</p></aside>
    </section>

