    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1689</span><span class="rf-text">The Bill of Rights forbids the English crown to suspend laws, levy taxes or keep a peacetime army without Parliament</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1701</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> fixes a Protestant succession by statute, later bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a> to the throne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1708</span><span class="rf-text">Queen Anne withholds assent from the Scottish Militia Bill, the last royal veto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1791</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> adopts the Constitution of 3 May, often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first modern written constitution</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1831</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>’s constitution declares that all powers emanate from the nation; Leopold I swears to uphold it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1889</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> creates an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> with an elected lower house under a sovereign emperor</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s new constitution makes the emperor “the symbol of the State and of the unity of the people”</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>’s constitution restores parliamentary monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos I</a> after Franco’s dictatorship</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy.jpg" alt="Constitutional monarchy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trekongem%C3%B8tet_1917_OB.F16064.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ludvig Forbech, CC BY-SA 3.0 no</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In a constitutional monarchy the monarch exercises authority according to a constitution and does not decide alone. The constitution may be a single document, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, or, as in the United Kingdom, a body of statutes, court decisions and conventions. In the parliamentary form, which most constitutional monarchies now follow, the monarch is head of state while a prime minister and cabinet, drawn from and answerable to an elected parliament, govern. The monarch appoints the prime minister, normally the leader who can command a majority, gives royal assent to laws, opens and dissolves parliament and formally makes treaties and appointments, but acts on the advice of ministers, who take political responsibility for each act. Continental constitutions make the point by requiring a minister’s countersignature on royal decrees; British law expresses it in the maxim that “the King can do no wrong”, since a minister answers instead. Courts, civil servants and armed forces act in the name of the crown.</p><p>Political scientists distinguish two types. In executive or semi-constitutional monarchies, such as Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, Kuwait, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, Bhutan, Monaco and Liechtenstein, the monarch keeps substantial powers to appoint and dismiss ministers, veto laws or dissolve the assembly, and the cabinet may answer to both crown and parliament. In ceremonial monarchies, which writers such as H. G. Wells called “crowned republics”, among them the United Kingdom and the other Commonwealth realms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the Scandinavian kingdoms, Spain and Japan, the monarch has little or no personal discretion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Japan have gone furthest, no longer making the monarch even the nominal head of the executive.</p><p>The ceremonial monarch is not wholly powerless. In The English Constitution (1867) Walter Bagehot named three rights left to the sovereign: “the right to be consulted, the right to encourage, the right to warn”. In the Commonwealth realms the monarch and the governors-general keep reserve powers for constitutional crises. What separates the form from absolute monarchy is that the crown is bound by law; what separates it from a parliamentary republic is chiefly the title and hereditary succession of the head of state, together with privileges such as the royal prerogative and sovereign immunity. The political scientist Vernon Bogdanor sums up the constitutional monarch as “a sovereign who reigns but does not rule”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="James II of England" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James II of England</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_II_by_Peter_Lely.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">School of Peter Lely, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kings who shared power with assemblies are far older than the term. Hittite kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> consulted an assembly called the panku, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> relates that in the mid-6th century BCE the lawgiver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demonax-of-mantinea">Demonax of Mantinea</a> left King Battus III of Cyrene only his royal estates and priestly offices. Medieval kingship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> was limited by custom, by the Church and by the estates of the realm: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> of 1215 bound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> to rule according to law, and the English Parliament, which took shape under Edward I, won the right to consent to taxation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> commended a mixed regime combining kingship with rule by the best and by the many, and Sir John Fortescue, writing around 1470, contrasted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, a “political and royal” lordship whose king could neither change the laws nor tax without his subjects’ assent, with the purely royal rule of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The modern form grew out of the constitutional conflicts of 17th-century England. In his Answer to the Nineteen Propositions of 1642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a> himself described the English constitution as a mixture of monarchy, aristocracy and democracy in King, Lords and Commons, but his attempt to rule without Parliament ended in civil war and his execution in 1649. After the Restoration of 1660, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James II of England</a>, a Catholic, prorogued Parliament in 1685 and used his prerogative to suspend the laws against Catholics and Protestant dissenters. In November 1688 William of Orange, stadtholder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, landed with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army; James fled to France, and in February 1689 a Convention Parliament declared that he had abdicated and offered the crown jointly to William III and his wife Mary II. The Declaration of Right, enacted in December as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a>, forbade the crown to suspend laws, levy taxes or keep a standing army in peacetime without Parliament’s consent, and required free elections and freedom of speech in Parliament. The Coronation Oath Act bound the monarchs to govern “according to the statutes in Parliament agreed on”, and the Claim of Right Act 1689 placed similar limits on the crown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>’s Two Treatises of Government, published in 1689 though largely written earlier, grounded government in the consent of the governed and made the legislature the supreme power of the commonwealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>’s The Spirit of the Laws (1748) held up the English constitution, with its separation of legislative, executive and judicial power, as the model of political liberty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-walpole.jpg" alt="Robert Walpole" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert-Walpole-1st-Earl-of-Orford.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Studio of Jean-Baptiste van Loo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> power passed by degrees from the crown to ministers answerable to Parliament. From 1698 the Civil List left the monarch dependent on Parliament for income, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a> fixed a Protestant succession, which brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a> to the throne in 1714. Queen Anne’s refusal of assent to the Scottish Militia Bill in 1708 was the last royal veto. Under the Hanoverian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Sir Robert Walpole</a>, in office from 1721 to 1742, became in effect the first prime minister, yet monarchs still shaped policy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>’s opposition to Catholic emancipation forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> to resign in 1801. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iv-of-the-united-kingdom">William IV of the United Kingdom</a> was the last monarch to dismiss a prime minister, Lord Melbourne, in 1834, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>’s personal power waned during her reign.</p><p>On the Continent the form arrived with the age of revolutions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>, often called the first modern written constitution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> adopted one of its own in 1791. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> granted France a Charter in 1814, and the July Monarchy of 1830 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> “King of the French” rather than “King of France”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> constitution of 1814 is still in force, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>’s of 1831, which declared that “all powers emanate from the nation”, became a model for others. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> introduced ministerial responsibility in 1848 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-rudolf-thorbecke">Johan Rudolf Thorbecke</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statuto-albertino">Statuto Albertino</a>, granted in Piedmont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> the same year, became the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> of 1871, however, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> rejected the British model: the chancellor answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>, not to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>.</p><p>The form spread beyond Europe. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> received a constitution in 1876, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> under a sovereign emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>’s Constitutional Revolution of 1905–1911 produced a parliament, and the Siamese Revolution of 1932 ended absolute monarchy in Siam. The British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1867, became self-governing constitutional monarchies under the crown. The world wars destroyed many thrones: the Russian, German and Austrian monarchies fell in 1917–1918, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, whose king had made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> prime minister in 1922, voted to become a republic in 1946. Japan’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan">constitution of 1947</a> made the emperor “the symbol of the State and of the unity of the people”. The surviving monarchies of western Europe adopted the British model, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> returned to monarchy under its constitution of 1978, after the dictatorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel.jpg" alt="Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jakob_Schlesinger_-_Hegel_1831.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jakob Schlesinger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defenders of constitutional monarchy have argued above all that it separates the dignity of the state from the business of government. Walter Bagehot divided the English constitution into “dignified” parts, which win the loyalty and reverence of the people, and “efficient” parts, which govern: the monarchy supplied the first so that the cabinet could do the second, and attachment to a family on the throne lent stability to ministries that changed with elections. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel</a>, in his Elements of the Philosophy of Right (1820), argued that a hereditary monarch with limited powers best embodied the unity of the state and gave it continuity in emergencies, because a crown that passed by birth was no prize for factions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, in his Reflections on the Revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (1790), defended the Revolution of 1688 as “made to preserve our ancient indisputable laws and liberties”, a model of change that kept inherited institutions.</p><p>Supporters also point to the form’s record. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> moved from royal government to parliamentary democracy without revolution after 1688, and the Scandinavian kingdoms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> extended the vote and built welfare states under the crown. The sociologist Seymour Martin Lipset observed in Political Man (1960) that most of the stable democracies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the English-speaking world were monarchies, and argued that keeping the crown had helped conservative groups to accept democratic change. Monarchs have at times defended constitutional government. On 23 February 1981, when officers of the Civil Guard seized the Spanish parliament, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos I</a> appeared on television in uniform to order the army to uphold the constitution, and the attempted coup collapsed. Supporters of the Australian Governor-General’s dismissal of the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gough-whitlam">Gough Whitlam</a> in 1975, after the Senate had blocked the budget, saw it as proof that reserve powers could break a deadlock and check politicians who exceeded their powers.</p><p>Advocates also value a head of state who stands outside party competition. A monarch trained for the role, serving for decades and owing the office to no party can, they argue, represent the whole nation, embody continuity across changes of government and act as a neutral arbiter when no party commands a majority, as the monarchs of Belgium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> do when they appoint a figure to lead negotiations for a coalition after an election.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii.jpg" alt="Victor Emmanuel III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitorio_Emanuel_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mario Nunes Vais, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Republicans have attacked the hereditary principle itself. Thomas Paine’s Common Sense (1776) denounced hereditary succession as an imposition on posterity and declared one honest man worth more to society “than all the crowned ruffians that ever lived”; in Rights of Man (1791) he argued that the idea of hereditary legislators was as absurd as that of a hereditary mathematician. Critics since have held that inherited office is incompatible with equal citizenship, that royal households are costly, and that the immunities of the crown, from prosecution, from some taxation and from some laws, place a family above the law. In theory the British monarch may still refuse assent to any bill, a power last used in 1708.</p><p>A second objection is that reserve powers are ill defined and can be used in partisan ways. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gough-whitlam">Gough Whitlam</a>’s supporters regarded his dismissal in 1975 as an abuse of the crown’s powers, and the crisis strengthened the movement for an Australian republic, though a referendum in 1999 rejected the model put to voters. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> Leopold III’s surrender to the German army in 1940, against his government’s advice, divided the country in the “Royal Question” and led to his abdication in 1951. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the clash between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-greece">Constantine II of Greece</a> and the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">George Papandreou</a> in 1965 opened a period of instability that ended in the colonels’ coup of 1967, and in 1974 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> voted to abolish the monarchy.</p><p>Where the monarch keeps real power, the form can cover rule that voters cannot hold to account. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> the chancellor needed no parliamentary majority, and the decisions of July 1914 were taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>, his ministers and his generals beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a>’s control; the system fell with defeat in 1918. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> in 1922 and signed the laws that turned the kingdom into a fascist dictatorship, which discredited the monarchy and led to its abolition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s own history offers examples of royal interference, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>’s refusal to accept Catholic emancipation to Victoria’s refusal to accept ministers she disliked. Critics also argue that a ceremonial crown leaves real power with a prime minister who exercises the old royal prerogatives, such as making treaties and declaring war, without the checks a written constitution would impose; the Conservative politician Lord Hailsham warned in 1976 of an “elective dictatorship”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution.jpg" alt="Glorious Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_of_Orange_III_and_his_Dutch_army_land_in_Brixham,_1688.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johan Herman Isings, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Constitutional monarchy was the setting in which parliamentary government developed. Cabinet government, the office of prime minister, ministerial responsibility, a loyal opposition and the convention that the head of state acts on advice all took shape under the British crown, and A. V. Dicey’s Introduction to the Study of the Law of the Constitution, first published in 1885, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of Parliament the core of British constitutional law. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster system</a> these institutions spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and many other former colonies, including those that later became republics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a> also shaped later charters: its ban on “cruel and unusual punishments” reappears in the Eighth Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, and some of the framers of that constitution, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, may have conceived the president as an elected constitutional monarch in the 18th-century sense.</p><p>Of the world’s 43 monarchies, most are now constitutional in some sense. Fifteen, the Commonwealth realms, share King Charles III as their monarch. Slightly more than a quarter of the constitutional monarchies are in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, among them the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, Luxembourg, Monaco and Liechtenstein, while the two most populous are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Two, Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elective monarchies</a>. Bhutan adopted a written constitution in 2008, and the monarchs of Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, Kuwait and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> govern under constitutions that leave them wide powers.</p><p>Historians have judged the founding settlement in different ways. Thomas Babington Macaulay’s History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Revolution of 1688</a> as a consensual and bloodless triumph of English liberty and limited monarchy, the classic Whig account. Later historians have stressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> invasion behind it, as Jonathan Israel did, or, like Steven Pincus in 1688: The First Modern Revolution (2009), its violence and novelty, while Tim Harris has argued that its most radical idea was a contract between ruler and people. Political scientists now treat parliamentary monarchies and parliamentary republics as variants of one system, differing in the title and succession of the head of state rather than in how power is exercised.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Constitutional monarchy keeps a hereditary sovereign but places the crown under law, leaving government to ministers answerable to elected parliaments. Born of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bill-of-rights-1689">Bill of Rights 1689</a>, it spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the wider world in the 19th and 20th centuries and remains the form of most of the world’s surviving monarchies.</em></p>

